<% content_for :title, "SpecGuard" %>

<%= render UI::PageComponent.new do %>
  <%# The subtitle names the product's purpose (Project Goals: suite intelligence for suites at
      20,000+ tests) and deliberately claims no capability. It used to read "Semantic test
      telemetry and duplicate prevention" — prevention was demoted by the owner on 2026-08-06 and
      the Goals article now says it "is explicitly not what the product is for". Which of the
      capabilities are actually available is answered by the two panels below, not here: a tagline
      is the wrong place to draw that line, because a reader cannot tell availability from tense. %>
  <%= render UI::PageHeaderComponent.new(
        title: "SpecGuard",
        subtitle: "Suite intelligence for 20,000-test suites — built for AI agents first."
      ) %>

  <div class="grid gap-4 lg:grid-cols-2">
    <%= render UI::PanelComponent.new(title: "Sign in") do %>
      <div class="space-y-4">
        <p class="text-sm text-app-content-secondary">
          Humans sign in with GitHub. CI and agents authenticate with a repository-scoped API key
          instead — you can mint one once you have registered a repository.
        </p>

        <% if github_oauth_configured? %>
          <%# turbo: false is load-bearing. OmniAuth's request phase answers this POST with a 302 to
              github.com; Turbo drives button_to through fetch, and a fetch cannot follow a
              cross-origin redirect — the response is opaque, so the click silently does nothing.
              Opting the form out of Turbo makes it a native browser POST, which follows the
              redirect to GitHub normally. %>
          <%= button_to "Sign in with GitHub", github_auth_path,
                        form: { data: { turbo: false } },
                        class: UI::ButtonComponent.classes(variant: :primary) %>
        <% else %>
          <%= render UI::AlertComponent.new(tone: :warning, title: "GitHub OAuth is not configured") do %>
            Set <code>GITHUB_CLIENT_ID</code> and <code>GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET</code> (or the
            <code>github.client_id</code> / <code>github.client_secret</code> credentials) and
            restart the app. See <code>config/initializers/omniauth.rb</code>.
          <% end %>
        <% end %>
      </div>
    <% end %>

    <%# == Why this is two panels rather than one ==================================================
        The single "What SpecGuard answers" panel this replaces led with
        ["Is this behaviour already tested?", "Prevention — /check-intent"]. Both halves of that row
        were wrong and they compounded: the route is not mounted (`config/routes.rb` carries exactly
        `get "repository"` and `post "ingest"` under api/v1), and prevention is no longer the
        product — a visitor who believed the panel went looking for a 404.

        The correction is NOT to swap one unbuilt promise for the five suite-intelligence
        deliverables, because when this split was written four of them were equally unanswerable:
        `Ingest::RunRecorder` wrote `test_runs` and `test_run_shards` and nothing per test, so what
        a 20,000-example run persisted was two integers.

        That half has since shipped. `Ingest::ObservationRecorder` writes one `SpecObservation` per
        example, and the answers standing on those rows — slowest tests, heaviest spec files,
        heaviest spec directories — are readable off the dashboard and off `GET /api/v1/repository`,
        so by the rule below they belong in the first panel and have moved there. The per-test
        `layer` has since joined them: the recorder now stores the layer each annotated example
        DECLARES, and `latest_run.slowest_examples` serves it per row. What still has nothing behind
        it is duplicate clusters — and the per-AREA rollup of that layer, which is a different
        question from knowing one test's, and the one the second panel still names.

        So availability is the axis the page is split on, and the split is carried by the panel
        TITLES and by a warning at the top of the second one — never by a footnote or by a change of
        tense, which is what a hurried reader skips. The rule for anything added here: a row belongs
        in the first panel only if a signed-in user can go and read that figure off the dashboard or
        the API today.

        The telemetry half of the old panel survives in substance; it was directionally right.
     %>
    <%= render UI::PanelComponent.new(title: "What SpecGuard answers today", id: "answers-today") do %>
      <div class="space-y-3">
        <%= render UI::DefListComponent.new(rows: [
              # TWO ROWS RATHER THAN ONE, because they were one sentence and are two questions.
              # "Annotated share" is the adoption metric and answers what a HUMAN has declared; it
              # never answered how much of the suite SpecGuard can say anything about, which on a
              # suite that has never been annotated is most of it. See `DerivedIntent`.
              ["How much of the suite carries a written @intent?", "Annotated share, over the run's own total"],
              ["How much of it can SpecGuard read at all?", "Authored, derived from the test's own description, or neither"],
              ["Where does the wall-clock go?", "Per run and per shard"],
              ["Which tests are slow, and which areas dominate the clock?", "Per test, per spec file and per directory"],
              ["Is the suite growing, and how fast?", "Run over run, per branch"],
              ["Can an agent read that without a browser?", "GET /api/v1/repository"]
            ]) %>

        <p class="text-xs text-app-muted">
          Every figure is read off runs your CI has posted to <code>POST /api/v1/ingest</code>.
          SpecGuard does not run your tests, and ingestion never fails a build.
        </p>
      </div>
    <% end %>
  </div>

  <%# The `id:` on this panel and on the one above is load-bearing, not decoration: the guard in
      `spec/requests/home_page_spec.rb` has to assert that the "Not available yet" warning sits
      INSIDE this panel, and a text window cut out of the response body cannot express containment
      at any length. Same `id:` + `Capybara.string(...).find` convention the dashboard panels use. %>
  <%= render UI::PanelComponent.new(title: "What SpecGuard is being built to answer", id: "roadmap") do %>
    <div class="space-y-4">
      <%# Leading with the warning rather than closing on it, because this panel's rows read exactly
          like the ones above and the whole point is that they are not the same kind of claim. %>
      <%= render UI::AlertComponent.new(tone: :warning, title: "Not available yet") do %>
        This is the product's direction, not its feature list. Ingestion does now record a row per
        example, and the timing answers built on those rows are in the panel above — as is the layer
        each annotated test declares, which ingestion now stores and the API now serves, and as is
        the suite-wide clustering, which `GET /api/v1/repository?near_duplicates=` now serves behind
        an opt-in ask. What is left here is blocked on work that has not been done rather than on
        data alone: rolling that clustering into a dashboard panel, and rolling that per-test layer
        up into a per-area picture. No panel answers these today.
      <% end %>

      <%# NUMBERED BY WHAT RENDERS, and that is a correction rather than a style choice. This comment
          used to count a fourth row that had already moved to `#answers-today` — "Which tests are
          slow, and which areas dominate the clock?", gone when the per-test write path shipped — so
          its "row 3" and "row 4" named the rows a reader counts as 2 and 3, and the instruction at
          the foot of this comment sent that reader to the wrong pair. The departed row is described
          here, but it is no longer COUNTED: the numbers below are the rendered ones.

          Row 1's rationale is narrowed to the half that is still genuinely open, the same way
          Row 2's below is: suite-wide clustering now EXISTS — `GET /api/v1/repository?near_duplicates=`
          serves it, opt-in, via `NearDuplicateClusters` — so the blocker this row names is no
          longer the clustering itself but the dashboard PANEL over it. The row stays here, and
          stays a "Needs ...", because no panel exists and moving it to `#answers-today` would be
          the same checkable-claim-that-does-not-hold defect this panel was edited to remove: the
          rule at the top of the first panel is that a row belongs there only if a signed-in user
          can read the figure off the dashboard or the API today — an agent can, via the endpoint,
          but a signed-in human browsing the dashboard cannot, and this panel's rows are the
          questions a person reads off the dashboard.

          Row 2's rationale is narrowed AGAIN, to the half that is still genuinely open. It used to
          read "which ingestion does not read", and that went false when `Ingest::ObservationRecorder`
          began storing `intent_layer` — the layer each annotated example declares is now persisted
          and served per row by `latest_run.slowest_examples`. What is still missing is the ROLLUP:
          knowing one test's layer is not knowing which layers already cover an AREA, and no panel
          and no endpoint groups the stored layers that way. That is the blocker this row now names.

          That rationale has to stay phrased as a BLOCKER ("Needs ..."), not as a status report,
          because the last row's "both of the above" resolves to the two needs printed above it. A
          rationale that opens by naming something which already ships would leave row 3 pointing at
          a shipped capability — the same checkable-claim-that-does-not-hold defect this panel was
          edited to remove. That is exactly why storing the layer did NOT promote this row to
          `#answers-today`: the row has to stay here, and stay a "Needs ...", for row 3 to keep
          resolving to two. Re-read row 3 against row 2 whenever either is reworded. %>
      <%= render UI::DefListComponent.new(rows: [
            ["Which groups of tests are potentially redundant?", "Needs a dashboard panel over the clustering"],
            ["What already exists for this area, and at which layers?", "Needs the stored per-test layer rolled up per area"],
            ["Which of those duplicates is safe to collapse?", "Needs both of the above"]
          ]) %>
    </div>
  <% end %>
<% end %>
